# Go sources are stored with LF. Without this pin, core.autocrlf=true checks
# them out as CRLF on Windows and `gofmt -l .` reports every file as
# unformatted — making the format gate fire permanently on line-ending noise
# rather than on real drift.
*.go text eol=lf

# The Go toolchain rewrites these as LF on every touch, so under
# core.autocrlf=true git reports them permanently modified while their content
# is byte-identical. Reviewing the go.sum diff is the mitigation for dependency
# substitution — a pair of files that always shows as modified is exactly where
# a real dependency change would hide.
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf

# Step 3 test fixtures are compared by exact byte offset, so their bytes must
# survive checkout unaltered on every platform. `-text` disables all line-ending
# conversion. Without it, core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF to CRLF on a Windows
# checkout and every one of the 22 spans in expected.json shifts by one byte per
# preceding line — a failure that reads as a segmentation bug rather than as a
# checkout artefact. The test asserts the fixture's SHA-256 before comparing so
# that this fails loudly if the pin is ever removed.
internal/core/domain/segment/testdata/demo.md -text
internal/core/domain/segment/testdata/expected.json -text

# A.1-E gap specifications, gold set and probe. Same reason as the Step 3
# fixtures above, one capability along. MANIFEST.md pins thirteen source
# documents by SHA-256, and GOLD_SET_v0.1.md carries character offsets into
# probe/manuscript_normalised.txt — G6 sits at 52791, roughly 800 lines in.
# Under core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout rewrites LF to CRLF, every hash
# in the manifest stops matching, and every gold offset shifts by one byte per
# preceding line. Both failures are silent: the hashes read as "wrong file"
# and the offsets read as an extraction bug rather than as a checkout artefact.
specs/** -text

# Review evidence. This is the directory the whole protocol is about, and it was
# the one directory not pinned. Every file under it is hashed by something:
# target.sha256 over target.json, capture-log.json's authoritative_sha256 over
# codex-output-raw.md, MC-2 check 10 binding codex-input.md to the frozen target,
# and each cycle's artifacts/ snapshot against the values recorded at freeze.
#
# Under core.autocrlf=true a checkout rewrites LF to CRLF and every one of those
# hashes stops matching at once. Cycle 01 would go INVALID and its eighteen
# findings would disappear from the loop state, which is the same failure the
# snapshot-at-freeze repair exists to prevent, arriving by a different route.
# Nothing would say "line endings"; it would read as fabricated or corrupted
# evidence.
#
# Found by git itself, warning on the first write into runs/ after this file was
# last edited: "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it".
runs/** -text

# Orchestration scripts are normalised to LF for the same reason go.mod and
# go.sum are: they get hashed. The bootstrap review freezes validate_cycle.py
# and its negative controls as a review target with a recorded SHA-256, and
# under core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout would rewrite them and that hash
# would stop verifying. `text eol=lf` rather than `-text`, matching the Go
# treatment above — normalise, do not merely decline to convert.
*.py text eol=lf
